Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:22.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  europe's largest economy will be  expecting a lot from angela merkel's  successor  so  what's on the to-do list  [Music]  climate change has played a bigger role  in germany's election than it might have  done in other years or in other  developed countries that in part is  because of the huge destruction caused  by flooding a couple of months ago  they killed nearly 200 people and left  many missing  [Music]  but it's not just about floods a  citizens assembly of 160 people was  commissioned to advise on what climate  ambitions germany should incorporate  into its public policy  the assembly backed plans for a coal  phase-out by 2030 a ban on internal  combustion car engines by the same year  and compulsory installation of solar  panels on new buildings  most germans believe the country and the  eu are simply not doing enough on  climate protection but implementing  those new climate policies will have a  big impact for instance germany still  leans heavily on coal to power its  world-famous factories  this won't be easy for the new  chancellor  germany has played a key leadership role  in the eu so maintaining and shaping  that sense of european unity will be  crucial for the next chancellor but  germans are beginning to have doubts the  eu's vaccination program was slow to get  going and one recent study found that a  striking 55 of germans believe the eu's  political system is broken  that figures up by a quarter from just  12 months before  there have been tensions between the  northern and southern european countries  about who should be paying for europe's  post-pandemic reconstruction the new  chancellor will need to help calm those  strained tempers and at home  a familiar challenge  immigration remains a big issue for some  voters  merkel allowed in many many migrants the  new chancellor will have their own views  since 2015 china has become germany's  largest trading partner goods worth  nearly 213 billion euros were traded  between the two countries in  but the us's increasingly hostile  attitude towards china has put pressure  on germany's close relationship so far  it's refused to cease its booming trade  and under merkel it opted for the  concept of vandal de grandel or change  through trade  um  actually  [Music]  [Music]  as other countries criticize the  detention of weaker muslims and the  imposition of draconian security laws in  hong kong berlin remained largely silent  but that approach is becoming much  harder to defend  a poll in august found that 58 of  respondents wanted the government to  take a harder line against beijing to  defend its own interests  germany struck a deal with russia to  allow the construction of a pipeline  directly between the two countries to  increase supplies of natural gas to  europe but nordstrom 2 is highly  controversial america for instance  thinks the pipeline will make europe  ever more dependent on russia for its  supply of energy  ukraine and poland also upset it means  them losing out on lucrative transit  fees for the gas that currently flows  through their countries  construction of the 11 billion pipeline  has already been finished america has  grudgingly agreed not to sanction the  german company involved  it struck a deal with germany to instead  sanction russia if it uses the energy as  a means to gain geopolitical leverage  so as one of the world's leading  economies the international community is  watching germany's election closely 16  years of leadership by angela merkel has  been stable  and enduringly popular but the direction  of this country has been defined by her  individual decisions rather than any  coherent political philosophy  the new chancellor will have to come up  with their own vision of germany's place  in the world

